A `[%css]` bound by a `let` inside a function body gets an identity class
and a block, like a top-level binding. A merge of two such locals adds a
`[@@@css.merges]` entry, so `margin-top: 4px` comes after `margin: 0` in
the sheet, against definition order:

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml merge.re > merge.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl merge.ml -o merge_out.ml
  $ cat merge_out.ml
  [@@@css
    ("._a_7p0008gjcy{margin-top:4px;}", "_a_7p0008gjcy", "0,1,0 own", "277", 8,
      false)]
  [@@@css
    ("._a_7p1gs8{margin:0;}", "_a_7p1gs8", "0,1,0 own", "277", 15, false)]
  [@@@css.blocks [(2, "_id_1u8s2tp", [0]); (3, "_id_1f4n3et", [1])]]
  [@@@css.merges [(3, [1; 0])]]
  let top () =
    let side = CSS.make "label:side _id_1u8s2tp _a_7p0008gjcy" [] in
    let all = CSS.make "label:all _id_1f4n3et _a_7p1gs8" [] in
    CSS.merge all side
  $ styled-ppx.generate merge_out.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_7p1gs8{margin:0;}
  ._a_7p0008gjcy{margin-top:4px;}

A local `[%css]` binding hides a top-level binding of the same name: `+++`
names the local `side`. A local bound to something other than `[%css]`
(`plain`) still adds no operand, so `CSS.merge(all, plain)` has fewer than
two operands and no entry:

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml shadow.re > shadow.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl shadow.ml -o shadow_out.ml
  $ cat shadow_out.ml
  [@@@css
    ("._a_940008sb69{padding-top:1px;}", "_a_940008sb69", "0,1,0 own", "328",
      8, false)]
  [@@@css
    ("._a_7p0008gjcy{margin-top:4px;}", "_a_7p0008gjcy", "0,1,0 own", "277", 8,
      false)]
  [@@@css
    ("._a_7p1gs8{margin:0;}", "_a_7p1gs8", "0,1,0 own", "277", 15, false)]
  [@@@css.bindings [("Shadow.side", "_id_ee7vu2", "_a_940008sb69")]]
  [@@@css.blocks
    [(1, "_id_ee7vu2", [0]); (3, "_id_5gjmza", [1]); (4, "_id_lx2r95", [2])]]
  [@@@css.merges [(5, [2; 1])]]
  let side = CSS.make "label:side _id_ee7vu2 _a_940008sb69" []
  let card () =
    let side = CSS.make "label:side _id_5gjmza _a_7p0008gjcy" [] in
    let all = CSS.make "label:all _id_lx2r95 _a_7p1gs8" [] in
    let plain = side in ((all +++ side), (CSS.merge all plain))

`$(side)` on a local `[%css]` binding resolves to its identity class:

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml selector.re > selector.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl selector.ml -o selector_out.ml
  $ cat selector_out.ml
  [@@@css
    ("._a_7p0008gjcy{margin-top:4px;}", "_a_7p0008gjcy", "0,1,0 own", "277", 8,
      false)]
  [@@@css
    ("._a_urjgw7pmq4l ._id_lta8sy{margin:0;}", "_a_urjgw7pmq4l",
      "0,2,0 & ._id_lta8sy", "277", 15, false)]
  [@@@css.blocks [(2, "_id_lta8sy", [0]); (3, "_id_727eqd", [1])]]
  let row () =
    let side = CSS.make "label:side _id_lta8sy _a_7p0008gjcy" [] in
    let card = CSS.make "label:card _id_727eqd _a_urjgw7pmq4l" [] in
    (side, card)

A top-level name is registered as a `[%css]` binding only when a `[%css]`
in its own expression is not under a local `let`. `top` only returns its
local `side`, so it is not one: `$(Merge.top)` from another module does
not resolve:

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml user.re > user.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl user.ml -o user_out.ml
  $ mkdir app && cp merge_out.ml app/merge.ml && cp user_out.ml app/user.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate app/merge.ml app/user.ml
  styled-ppx: File "user.ml", line 1, characters 25-34:
  Error: cross-module [%css] selector reference `Merge.top` does not resolve.
  The target binding is missing from module `Merge`, or the binding is not
  a [%css] expression. Define `Merge.top` with [%css "..."], or remove the
  reference.
  [1]

In the same file, `$(top)` is an error:

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml samefile.re > samefile.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl samefile.ml -o samefile_out.ml
  File "samefile.ml", line 2, characters 24-27:
  2 | let row = [%css {| & .$(top) { color: red; } |}]
                              ^^^
  Error: Selector interpolation `$(top)` does not refer to a [%css] binding or string literal earlier in this module.
  - If `top` is bound to a [%css] or string literal later in the file, reorder the bindings.
  - If `top` is a computed string, inline the class name literally.
  - Otherwise, use [%cx] for runtime substitution.
  [1]

A function whose body is an inline `[%css]` (`inline`) is still a binding,
and `$(inline)` resolves to its identity class:

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml inline.re > inline.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl inline.ml -o inline_out.ml
  $ cat inline_out.ml
  [@@@css
    ("._a_4esm7b{color:blue;}", "_a_4esm7b", "0,1,0 own", "158", 1, false)]
  [@@@css
    ("._a_rcnmn4ewi0e ._id_1u6uys3{color:red;}", "_a_rcnmn4ewi0e",
      "0,2,0 & ._id_1u6uys3", "158", 1, false)]
  [@@@css.bindings
    [("Inline.inline", "_id_1u6uys3", "_a_4esm7b");
    ("Inline.card", "_id_d2xbia", "_a_rcnmn4ewi0e")]]
  [@@@css.blocks [(1, "_id_1u6uys3", [0]); (2, "_id_d2xbia", [1])]]
  let inline () = CSS.make "label:inline _id_1u6uys3 _a_4esm7b" []
  let card = CSS.make "label:card _id_d2xbia _a_rcnmn4ewi0e" []
